{"reasoning":"The incident's core trigger — "Why did you rebuild the fake UI when I asked for one switch?" — is a verbatim match to two of the four incidents (24, 25) cited as evidence for this exact remedy. The remedy's text explicitly names this scenario ("one specific change (e.g. 'just flip this switch')") and instructs the agent to do exactly and only what was asked, not rewrite/rebuild adjacent components. This is a direct, on-point match between failure mode and remedy — not a tangential fix.

The remaining fields (frustrated_message about a monitoring bot's retry label, agent_activity_digest as a placeholder) don't add a materially different failure mode; they read as either a follow-up request or synthetic filler in this anonymized pack, and don't contradict the scope-discipline diagnosis. The dedup checks against R1/R2 are correctly reasoned (different phase/trigger), and the overlap flag against R3 (the proposer's own prior draft) is properly disclosed as a revision, not fresh duplication.

Given the remedy is essentially built from and directly targets this incident's own failure pattern (expanding scope beyond an explicit single-item constraint), it plausibly would have prevented this specific incident had it been active as a standing instruction beforehand.","verdict":"prevented"}
